+### The alias flush on every encoder's end barrier
+
+- **Symptom:** Metal command allocators retain large allocations after submissions containing many encoders.
+- **Cause:** `m4_end` used `resource_vis()` on producer barriers as well as consumer barriers. `MTL4VisibilityOptionResourceAlias` on every producer increases command memory even though the following consumer already flushes aliases.
+- **Fix:** producer barriers use device visibility. Consumer barriers retain alias visibility; copy encoders that wrote through an aliased buffer retain their alias producer flush. `DM_NVN_END_ALIAS=1` restores the old behavior and `ab` alternates it with `DM_AB` (adapted from upstream `5b84d37`). Ordering barriers remain.
+- **Check:** `t_end_alias` compares 600 production render passes with a control retaining the alias consumer flush and a device-only producer. On the local M4, allocation falls from 39,462,072 to 8,398,008 bytes, equal to the control. `t_copy_alias` checks shader and copy dependencies through distinct buffers over the same storage in direct and deferred modes. iPhone frame-rate benefit is a separate measurement.
+- **Found on:** [The Legend of Zelda: Ocarina of Time](../../dm/games/0400486024DEC000/README.md).
